**Definition 1.3 (Passive frame and spectral coordinates).** Put

$$
\begin{gathered}
r=\sqrt{17},\qquad D=\operatorname{diag}(1,i,i,i,1),\qquad
A=-iD^\dagger KD,\qquad O(t)=e^{tA}=D^\dagger U(t)D,\\
\widehat T(X)=D^\dagger T(DXD^\dagger)D,\qquad
Q_J(t)=O(t)^T\Pi_JO(t),\qquad \Pi_J=\sum_{s\in J}\Pi_s,\\
B=-A^2,\quad \mu_\pm=(5\pm r)/2,\quad \omega_\pm=\sqrt{\mu_\pm},\\
R_\pm=\frac{B(B-\mu_\mp I)}{\mu_\pm(\mu_\pm-\mu_\mp)},\qquad
R_0=I-R_+-R_-=dd^T,\qquad d=(e_2-e_5)/\sqrt2,\\
u_\pm=\frac{R_\pm e_N}{\sqrt{(1\pm1/r)/2}},\qquad
v_\pm=Au_\pm/\omega_\pm,\qquad
w_\pm(\alpha)=\cos\alpha\,u_\pm+\sin\alpha\,v_\pm.
\end{gathered}
\tag{1.4}
$$

Sector order is $(0,+,-)$, with dimensions $(d_0,d_+,d_-)=(1,2,2)$.
In the coordinate order $L=(N,25)$ and $V=(2,3,5)$,

$$
A=\begin{pmatrix}0&C\\-C^T&0\end{pmatrix},\quad
C=\begin{pmatrix}1&1&1\\1&0&1\end{pmatrix},\quad
CC^T=\begin{pmatrix}3&2\\2&2\end{pmatrix}.
\tag{1.5}
$$

Thus the nonzero squared frequencies solve $x^2-5x+2=0$.
The mutually orthogonal projections are explicitly

$$
\begin{aligned}
R_+|_L&=\begin{pmatrix}(r+1)/(2r)&2/r\\2/r&(r-1)/(2r)\end{pmatrix},\\
R_+|_V&=\begin{pmatrix}
(r+3)/(4r)&1/r&(r+3)/(4r)\\
1/r&(r-3)/(2r)&1/r\\
(r+3)/(4r)&1/r&(r+3)/(4r)
\end{pmatrix},\\
R_0|_L&=0,\qquad
R_0|_V=\frac12\begin{pmatrix}1&0&-1\\0&0&0\\-1&0&1\end{pmatrix},
\qquad R_-=I-R_0-R_+.
\end{aligned}
\tag{1.6}
$$

All cross blocks vanish. The two plane bases $(u_\pm,v_\pm)$ are
orthonormal. Define

$$
\begin{gathered}
G(\alpha,\beta)=R_0+\cos\alpha R_++\sin\alpha AR_+/\omega_+
+\cos\beta R_-+\sin\beta AR_-/\omega_-,\\
\mathsf J=\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1,1),\qquad
O(t)=G(\omega_+t,\omega_-t).
\end{gathered}
\tag{1.7}
$$

Here $\mathsf Jd=-d$, $\mathsf Ju_\pm=u_\pm$,
$\mathsf Jv_\pm=-v_\pm$, and $\mathsf J A\mathsf J=-A$.
Let $\mathcal G$ be the compact orthogonal group generated by these
two-circle rotations and $\mathsf J$, with normalized Haar measure.
The angles and $D$ are mathematical design coordinates, not extra
future controls. Transposes below refer to this frame; a map dagger
denotes its trace-pairing adjoint.

